\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I don't know if I can give you the single most useful question for understanding.\'a0 But I can give you a few useful questions you are welcome to reflect on in light of the text and what the text demands.\
The dating scheme used in the first verses is different from that used earlier in the book.\'a0 In that news would take time to travel in the ancient world, the clear dating, when read later, will reveal that divine revelation as such.\'a0 But is that all that is going on?\
You've traced one of the earlier texts which the blood of chapter 24 references.\'a0 But in what way does Ezekiel use these references to communicate?\'a0 What details of the text push us to consider more deeply what Ezekiel is saying through them?\
You say 'but then', but what is the link between this blood city, and the pot metaphor?\'a0 How does Ezekiel use that link in his rhetoric?\
That corruption has two ways we can read it. Either the metal of the pot is corrupt, or the meat within the pot.\
The meat within reads the preposition 'in' more naturally, and does not require a focus shift in our reading.\
However, in verse 11, the metal of the pot clearly needs cleansed - thus the extreme heating of the copper. This is more natural if the corruption has been in the metal of the pot all along, but contamination going beyond the physical from rotting meat is at least a logical possibility. And whether we read pouring as pouring out melted metal, or pouring out the corrupted meat, remains a parallel interpretive question.\
Either way we read the corruption, the corruption is stubborn, and extreme measures must be taken with the pot, that is, Jerusalem.\
So first, yes, how ought we read the corruption?\'a0 But second, on either reading, what is the rhetorical intent of bringing in the corruption metaphor?\'a0 What is Ezekiel communicating, given what we know of the original audience, and how that communication must fit in with other things Ezekiel is saying?\
As for setting the blood on bare rock, this is indeed shameless public guilt, and a connection to the bloody city of chapter 22 with its breadth of predatory disregard for human life. And as you note, there is also a reference here to Leviticus 17, with its command to pour out the blood of animals to be eaten and cover it with earth. This goes beyond eating animals in the correct way, because of the rationale for the command: the blood is its life. Whether murder, or economic injustice, or child sacrifice, the issue is the same, that blood represents life which is God's alone, and which will cry out to God if taken unjustly.\
Within the analogy of the cooking pot of meat, the contents of the pot are like meat with the blood still in it; they may not be eaten, and it is too late to turn them into something that may be eaten.\
But you are quite correct to note the two causes, the two placements, of the blood on the rock.\'a0 Your interpretive question, dual agency or making visible, is a good one.\'a0 But I think to answer that question, we should ask another one.\'a0 What does the text push us to know and take into account about the LORD's purposes through this placement?\
I've got to go to the text carefully myself over verse 12.\'a0 This is one of the more difficult verses, and while what I say should always be carefully verified with the text, that both goes double with this verse, and yet is more difficult.\'a0 But I would translate this weariness as 'she has wearied' or 'she has caused to be weary'.\'a0 There may be some reference to the accusations of chapter 11.\'a0 There may be an assonance or the like which I am missing.\'a0 \
But, keeping in mind that I do not think we have properly established the corruption as rust as such, I do think you are right.\'a0 The overall point is clear.\'a0 The attempted purification fails.\'a0 Verse 11 seems to say the fire will indeed succeed in ending the corruption, and verse 13 says the cleansing will not happen until fury, fury that is likely much the same as the fire. But cleansing was offered and refused - thus, I think, giving us reason to believe the modal interpretation, God would have cleansed; but they would not be cleansed. The prophets Jeremiah and Isaiah had offered cleansing a hundred different ways. Good kings, Josiah, Hezekiah, and even further back had attempted to use royal power to cleanse at least some things. Torah offered commands, warnings, and means towards cleansing. But, verse 13's reference to lewdness reminds us of the history in chapters 16 and 23, the determination at all costs to be corrupt.\
But why does Ezekiel speak this way?\'a0 What role does this cleansing, earlier failed cleansing, lewdness and filth, fire and fury, play in Ezekiel's rhetoric?\
This next question is, perhaps, more important than the others so far, in that it guides interpretation of the rest: why is what Ezekiel is to say called a parable, and in what sense is parable meant?\'a0 How does it function as a parable?\'a0 Chapter 11 had a dispute between the LORD and the people over a saying they used.\'a0 Whether some in the audience continue to use a pot metaphor favorably to themselves, or merely continued in attitudes refuted in chapter 11, the 'rebellious house' to which verse 3 tells us these words are directed ought to control our reading of 'parable'.\
And then, more importantly still, how do verses 1 and 2 relate to verse 14?\'a0 In a way, all of verses 1 through 13 have been leading up to verse 14. Think through the implied relationships in the clauses of verse 14. 'I am the LORD' - how is this related to 'I have spoken'? 'it shall come to pass' - how is this related to 'I will do it'? 'I will not go back', 'I will not spare', and 'I will not relent' all seem to be parallel - what is their relationship with 'according to your ways and your deeds you will be judged'? And then, what is the relationship of all of these with the final 'the declaration of the Lord GOD'?
