\b \cf2 On the dating scheme:
\b0 \
My automatic thought was that the precise dating proves prophetic authenticity - news couldn't travel that fast, so Ezekiel knowing the siege began "this very day" demonstrates divine revelation.\
But that treats the dating as apologetic device rather than asking what it does rhetorically. Let me reconsider.\
The phrase 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1462 \u1510 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1497 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1494 \u1462 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  appears three times in two verses - "this bone-day, this essential-day." The repetition is emphatic. And the command is 
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1464 \u1489 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  - "write for yourself." Not "proclaim" or "speak" but "write." This creates a permanent record that can be checked later.\
But more than that: Ezekiel is being made a witness. The siege beginning is not something he learns about and reports. He is told by YHWH what is happening simultaneously in Jerusalem. He becomes, through divine revelation, present to both locations at once. The exiles with him hear a man in Babylon describe what the king of Babylon is doing to Jerusalem at that moment.\
This collapses the temporal and spatial distance the exiles might use to deflect. "This is far away" - no, it's happening now. "This is later" - no, it's today. "We don't really know what's happening there" - yes you do, because YHWH is telling you through his prophet.\
The dating scheme isn't just proof of prophecy; it's elimination of excuses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 On how Ezekiel uses the blood reference:
\b0 \
You're right that I traced the Leviticus 17 connection but didn't ask what Ezekiel is doing with it rhetorically.\
The detail that pushes deeper: the blood is on 
\f2 \uc0\u1510 \u1456 \u1495 \u1460 \u1497 \u1495 \u1463 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1505 \u1462 \u1500 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1  - "bareness of rock." Not just "rock" but rock that is bare, exposed, gleaming. The blood cannot possibly be hidden there. It sits visible, exposed to sun and sky.\
Leviticus 17:13 commands covering blood with dust because blood represents life (
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1462 \u1508 \u1462 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1 ). The covering is not merely hygiene but acknowledgment that life belongs to God. To leave blood exposed is to treat life as if it doesn't matter, as if no one is watching, as if there's no one to cry out to.\
But Jerusalem didn't just fail to cover - she placed it on bare rock. Deliberately. Brazenly. As if to say: let it cry out. We don't care. No one will answer.\
And then YHWH says: I placed it there too. 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1492 \u1463 \u1506 \u1458 \u1500 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1461 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1511 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1511 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  - "to bring up wrath, to take vengeance."\
The rhetorical force: Jerusalem thought she was displaying contempt for the blood's cry. YHWH was ensuring the evidence would remain visible for judgment. Her brazenness becomes her condemnation. What she meant as defiance, God meant as exhibit A.\
This is similar to chapter 22's point about bloodshed encompassing all predatory disregard for life. The blood on the rock isn't just murder victims - it's everyone whose life was treated as expendable for others' gain. And it's all on display.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

