\b \cf2 On how to read the corruption:
\b0 \
You've given me reason to reconsider my assumption that this is rust in the metal versus contamination from the meat.\
The preposition 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  in verse 6 (
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1462 \u1500 \u1456 \u1488 \u1464 \u1514 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  - "its corruption in it") is ambiguous - the corruption is "in" the pot, which could mean in the metal or in the contents.\
But I had jumped too quickly to "rust" as a way of making a theological point about corruption becoming essential rather than accidental. Let me look at what the text actually requires.\
Verse 11's heating of the empty pot does suggest the pot itself needs cleansing - you can't cleanse meat by heating an empty pot. So by verse 11, the corruption has affected the pot/vessel itself, whatever caused it.\
But this might be precisely Ezekiel's point: the contents were so corrupted (bloody meat) that the corruption spread to the vessel. The city that tolerated bloody contents has itself become corrupted. The distinction between "what Jerusalem contains" and "what Jerusalem is" has collapsed.\
The rhetorical intent: whether we read it as original rust or contamination spread from contents, the message is that Jerusalem cannot simply expel the wicked and continue. The pot itself is compromised. Even if all the corrupted meat were removed, the vessel that held it is now unfit.\
This answers those in chapter 11 who thought the deportation of others meant they were the protected remnant. No - you are the pot that held the corruption. Even if you think of yourselves as the vessel rather than the contents, the vessel is now corrupt.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
