\f1  - "she did not pour it on the ground to cover it with dust"\
The warning implicit: blood that isn't covered cries out. Genesis 4:10 - Abel's blood cries from the ground. By not covering, Jerusalem ensured the cry would continue. The warning: uncovered blood brings vengeance.\
Then verse 8: YHWH's placement ensures the blood remains uncovered 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1500 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1505 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 
\f1  - "so it would not be covered." The warning becomes judgment: what you refused to cover, I ensure stays visible.\
On the level of the pot metaphor:\
Within the cooking analogy, blood in meat makes it unfit. Leviticus prohibits eating blood. Meat with blood still in it cannot be properly eaten - it's corrupted. The pot's contents are like bloody meat - they cannot be redeemed, only removed.\
And bloody meat left in a pot would cause the pot to become foul. The blood-corruption of the contents explains why the pot itself needs such extreme cleansing.\
The three levels work together: accusation (you shed blood brazenly) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  warning (uncovered blood brings vengeance) 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  metaphor (bloody contents corrupt the vessel, all must be judged).\
