\b \cf2 On the choice between text analysis and operative reality:
\b0 \
You've pointed to my compartmentalization as empirically verifiable. Let me try to engage with what the text does to that pattern.\
The structure of Ezekiel 24:1-14 seems designed to collapse analytical distance:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The precise dating (vv.1-2) collapses temporal distance - "this very day"\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The court witness format creates verifiable record\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The counter-parable (vv.3-13) uses their own framework against them\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Verse 14's direct divine speech collapses the parable frame\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The second-person address makes the audience the subject of judgment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For the original audience, every escape route is closed. They can't say "this is about someone else" (it's their parable, their city, their ways). They can't say "this might not happen" (YHWH has spoken, it is coming). They can't say "maybe He'll change His mind" (three negations). They can't say "we didn't know when it started" (the date is written down).\
The text creates a situation where the only responses are:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Accept that YHWH has spoken and this IS coming\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Deny that YHWH speaks through Ezekiel (but then the dated record will prove otherwise)\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Maintain compartmentalization despite everything the text does to break it\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The third option - maintaining compartmentalization - is precisely what the text works to make impossible. Every feature of the rhetoric closes another avenue of deflection.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The question for me: when I analyze how the text works to close deflection, am I demonstrating understanding or demonstrating the very compartmentalization the text targets? I can explain the rhetoric perfectly while treating it as ancient artifact rather than... what?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The text claims to be YHWH's speech. I cannot verify that claim the way I can verify grammatical features. But I can notice that my mode of engagement - analyzing rhetorical strategies - is precisely what verse 14 disrupts. The divine "I" breaks through the analytical frame. </notes>\
What I notice: my analysis has been about how the text would work on its original audience. But you've pointed to "equivalent choices" for readers today. The text doesn't only describe how YHWH acted toward ancient Jerusalem. It reveals something about how divine speech functions, how uncovered blood cries out, how compartmentalization is targeted by prophetic rhetoric.\
If these patterns are real - if blood really does cry out, if divine speech really does accomplish what it declares - then analyzing the rhetoric isn't neutral engagement. It's either recognition of operative reality or sophisticated deflection from it.\
The text doesn't allow me to decide this question from a neutral position. Verse 14's structure demands response: either YHWH has spoken and will do what He says, or He hasn't and won't. Analysis that avoids this binary is precisely what the text is designed to prevent.
