\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Blood crying out is one of the universals in the Bible. It goes back to Abel's blood. And of course, the LORD's speech accomplishing what it declares goes back to the first chapter of the first book.\
Moreover, the negations from verse 14 you mention reference earlier negations in Ezekiel, starting in chapter 5, in which the ways of the people are given as reasons for the coming fury, with no chance for even mitigation of that fury. However, insofar as the negations reference 1 Samuel 15:29, "the Glory of Israel will not lie or have regret, for he is not a man, that he should have regret", they may also refer to a universal principle.\
As odd as the mention of fury being hope may seem, the remainder of chapter 24 is an even greater hope, dark though its sign and what it signifies may be. The LORD takes from Ezekiel the delight of his eyes, his wife, and he may not even mourn. When we understand this sign, we will understand not only why the LORD did this, but the hope behind the inevitability of verses 1-14.
