\cf2 But I'm uncertain whether I'm reading the hope correctly. The text doesn't say "and then they will repent and be restored." It says they will know. Is knowledge itself the hope? Or is there something more I'm missing?\
The parallel between Ezekiel's personal loss and the collective loss suggests YHWH entering into the grief with them. He takes from Ezekiel what He takes from them. Ezekiel bears in his body what they will experience. YHWH isn't distant from the suffering but orchestrates it through someone who suffers it first.\
Is that the hope - that YHWH is present even in the stripping away? That the loss is not abandonment but purposeful action aimed at recognition?
