\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I think that rotting away is key to our understanding, and I think you are on the right track. The word here in chapter 24 and the concept of rotting forms somewhat of an inclusio, a wrapping of the chapters in between, with chapter 4 and verse 17 of chapter 4. In calling back to chapter 4, that word may also be, in turn, calling back to the judgement warned of in Leviticus 26.\
Ezekiel has spoken before of recognition of the iniquities of the people in Jerusalem. But I think you are right that here, revelation of their guilt is one of the driving forces of the rhetoric. And mourning as if innocent sufferers may be one of the targets.\
That uncertainty you have though, must reach for the solid ground of the evidence in the text. What I say is no such solid ground except insofar as it can point to the text.\
With that in mind, I point to another interpretive key. We are told that Ezekiel here is a sign, as he was in chapter 12. Nothing you haven't just said - but reflection, not on what I think that sign means, or what others have said it means, but on how that might be an interpretive key when taking the text seriously, may help.\
Also, think perhaps of the nature of the restriction of speech on Ezekiel. What was he not allowed to do, and where else does this come up?
