\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1464 \u1492 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  - "I am your sign; as I have done, so it will be done to them."\
The sign precedes and demonstrates the reality. Ezekiel goes into exile before they do. Ezekiel loses the delight of his eyes before they do. Ezekiel cannot mourn before they cannot mourn.\
But here's what strikes me: Ezekiel is commanded not to mourn. The exiles, when the news comes, will be UNABLE to mourn because they're rotting in their iniquities. Ezekiel's obedient non-mourning signifies their guilty non-mourning. He does by command what they will do by incapacity.\
The sign makes their future experience visible now. When they see Ezekiel not mourning his wife's death, they're seeing what they will be - people who cannot properly grieve because their grief is entangled with their guilt.\
And when they see Ezekiel living through this and remaining YHWH's prophet, remaining obedient, remaining the vehicle of divine speech... perhaps that too is part of the sign? That someone can pass through this kind of loss and still be used by YHWH?\
