\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I am not sure the text shows that such an experience can be survived.\
But consider this. Chapter 4, the same chapter with that rotting near the end in the interpretation, had as part of the embodied sign Ezekiel enacted an iron wall between Ezekiel and the city, with Ezekiel's face against the city. Ezekiel, before the siege and during the siege, was setting his face against the city, as the LORD did. Through Ezekiel, the LORD communicated the reasons for the LORD's face against that city, and Ezekiel was limited to such communication; no reproving was permitted.\
And on that day, his mouth will be opened. Ezekiel is, I think, still a sign for the nation here, as he was when he put that iron wall up, as he explicitly was when he went as into exile through a hole in the wall, as he explicitly was when he did not mourn his wife.\
Moreover, verse 21 has much of its material paralleled in verse 25, leading into verse 26. \
So I think the text supports reading the sign of this chapter as hopeful in a way.\
But consider what it might mean for the people to be called to confession. Consider what it might mean for even the loss of their children will not be publicly mourned. Consider what it might mean for Ezekiel for the LORD to take his wife, the same as the LORD will take the stronghold.
