\b \cf2 On what it means for the people to be called to confession:
\b0 \
If Leviticus 26:40 is the trajectory - 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1514 \u1456 \u1493 \u1463 \u1491 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  - "and they will confess their iniquity" - what does that require?\
Confession isn't just feeling bad. The verb 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1491 \u1492 
\f1  in hithpael means to acknowledge, to declare openly. They must name what they did. They must own it as theirs, not as fate or as something done to them.\
The prohibition on mourning as innocent sufferers serves this. If they could mourn normally, they could process the loss as tragedy that befell them. But they must rot in their iniquities - must sit with the knowledge that they did this, that their ways and deeds brought this, that the blood on bare rock was their blood, brazenly displayed.\
Only from that place - not "we were victims" but "we did this" - can confession come.\
And Ezekiel with his opened mouth can then call them to that confession. Before, they wouldn't hear. Now, having lost everything and knowing why, they might.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 On even the loss of children not being publicly mourned:
\b0 \
This is harder.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1504 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1494 \u1463 \u1489 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1495 \u1462 \u1512 \u1462 \u1489 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1508 \u1465 \u1468 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  - "your sons and daughters whom you left behind will fall by the sword."\
They left their children in Jerusalem when they went into exile. Those children will die. And they cannot mourn them publicly.\
The phrase 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1494 \u1463 \u1489 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  - "whom you left behind" - carries weight. They abandoned their children. Not deliberately perhaps, but the exile separated them from their families. And now those families die.\
Can they mourn as bereaved parents when they themselves... what? When they themselves participated in the iniquities that brought this? When the blood on the rock included their own children sacrificed to idols (as chapter 23:37-39 accused)?\
The children dying by Babylonian sword are the same children whose siblings were slaughtered for idols and whose parents then entered the sanctuary "on that same day." The parents' mourning is compromised by their own bloodguilt.\
But also: the loss of children is total loss. It's the end of future. It's not just "we lost our temple" but "we lost our continuation." The exiles will have no one to carry on.\
Unless... the rotting in iniquity leads to confession leads to humbled hearts leads to YHWH remembering covenant. The future that seems cut off in chapter 24 is the future that chapter 37's valley of dry bones restores. "Can these bones live?"\
The unmourned children might be the ground from which the question arises: is there any hope at all? And the opened mouth might be what answers: yes, but not through your mourning, not through your continuity, only through YHWH's remembering.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
