\cf2 The exiles will experience 1 and 2. But 3 - will they experience something corresponding to Ezekiel's opened mouth?\
Perhaps. If they rot in their iniquities (as the sign demonstrates) but then confess (as Leviticus 26 opens), then something changes for them too. The barrier that prevented YHWH from hearing them (the iron wall of their rebellion) might come down when they stop defending and start confessing.\
The hope isn't that the loss is survivable in some stoic sense. The hope is that the loss creates conditions for something that wasn't possible before. The people couldn't confess while they had something to defend. Ezekiel couldn't reprove while they wouldn't listen. YHWH couldn't receive them while they held to their corrupted security.\
Now there's nothing to defend. Now they might listen. Now confession becomes possible. And on that day, the mouth opens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
