\cf2 What I remain uncertain about:\
The text seems to hold together judgment and hope in ways that resist my tendency to separate them. The hope isn't after the judgment or instead of the judgment but somehow within it - the judgment creates the conditions for what comes next.\
Is this a correct reading, or am I softening the severity? The text doesn't promise they will confess, only that they will rot in their iniquities. The opened mouth doesn't guarantee reception. Leviticus 26:40 describes what would need to happen, not what will happen.\
Perhaps the hope is real but not guaranteed. The path exists but must be walked. And Ezekiel's sign shows the path - through unmourned loss, through rotting in iniquity, toward something that wasn't possible before.
