\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  - "It is coming and I will do it"\
The same YHWH who spoke judgment through Moses also spoke restoration. The same principle - "I have spoken, it is coming, I will do it" - applies to both. If YHWH's word accomplishes what it declares, and YHWH has declared both curse and blessing, both exile and return, both profaning of the sanctuary and a future temple where His glory dwells...\
Then verse 14 isn't only sealing doom. It's establishing the principle by which hope is also guaranteed. The word that brings judgment is the same word that brings restoration. And that word does not return empty.\
Leviticus 26 doesn't just predict confession - it speaks it into the sequence. Deuteronomy 30:1-6 doesn't just hope for return - it declares that YHWH will circumcise their hearts. The prophets don't merely wish for restoration - they speak YHWH's word that creates what it announces.\
So when Ezekiel's mouth opens after the fall, he speaks into conditions where YHWH's restorative word can take effect. Not because the people have earned it by confessing - confession itself is part of what the word creates. The path must be walked, but the word makes the path real, makes the walking possible.\
