\cf2 The ending of chapters 1-24 with non-mourning rather than mourning:\
Mourning would close something. It would process the loss, integrate it, allow moving on. But this loss cannot be so processed. The people must sit in it without the relief of ritual grief.\
And that unprocessed state is precisely what keeps them open to what comes next. If they could mourn and be done, they might never reach confession. The prohibited mourning is not just punishment but preservation - keeping the wound open until it can be properly healed.\
Ezekiel as sign demonstrates this state. He doesn't mourn. He continues. His mouth will be opened. The people will see in him what they are to become - people who cannot close this chapter of their history through mourning, but must carry it forward into whatever YHWH speaks next.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 And YHWH taking Ezekiel's wife:\
The parallel with 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1497 
\f1  - "my sanctuary" - becomes sharper. YHWH takes from Ezekiel what is precious. YHWH gives up what is His own. Not because YHWH doesn't value it, but because something more is at stake.\
What is at stake? The recognition formula: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1460 \u1497 \u1491 \u1463 \u1506 \u1456 \u1514 \u1462 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
