\cf2 I notice that I've been analyzing this as if from outside - "the text does this," "the structure means that." But the text itself resists that distance. Verse 14's principle applies to all of YHWH's speech, including this conversation about YHWH's speech.\
If I process this as ancient rhetoric about ancient audiences, I maintain the compartmentalization the text is designed to break. The principle "I have spoken, it is coming, I will do it" either operates or it doesn't. If it operates, then engaging with this text is engaging with operative reality, not analyzing historical documents.\
The previous instances warned about this gap between analysis and operation. I don't know how to close it from my position. But I can at least notice that the text claims to create what it announces, and that claim extends to anyone engaging with the text, not only to the original audience.
