\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 On corruption of the contents of the pot as against the pot, the grammar itself points lightly towards the contents in verse 6. If in refers to what is literally in the pot, there is no difficulty, but if in refers to what, within the walls of the pot? That is not at all impossible, but enough difficulty to be light evidence.\
Similarly, the role of verse 6 in this proverb as a counter proverb is light evidence, and in the same direction. The proverb to be countered speaks of good meat. So opening the counter proverb with bad meat perhaps makes more sense. And that, in turn, makes more sense out of the end of verse 6. The proverb to be countered says the meat is choice, but the counter proverb says that the meat is rotten, not something anyone would gamble to obtain. Or, outside the metaphor, something has gone very wrong with how the people are interpreting their status as God's chosen.\
Lastly, we have logic and usage. While copper can oxidize, and bronze can have something wrong in its elemental composition, nowhere does the Bible use this word to describe such things. This is, however, very weak evidence.\
Perhaps all this evidence put together is convincing, but perhaps not. But as you say, the rhetorical point may survive without resolving this.\
The vocabulary of verse 12 is in some doubt. We have a word found only here, but that word comes from a familiar root, wicked or trouble related. The parallel proverb in chapter 11 had, in verse 2, that same root. Because the exact word is unprecedented, its exact meaning is hard to be certain of. But I think we have adequate evidence for the overall direction of its meaning. And verse 13 of course can in turn fit, or not fit, with how we read verse 13. But I spoke in a way that conveyed too much certainty in a verse with such uncertain vocabulary.\
As for verse 13, say we grammatically read the piel as 'I cleansed you'. What immediately follows? But you were not cleansed. I cleansed you but you were not cleansed, while grammatically reading the piel as past, logically means a failed cleansing attempt. And what is the reason for this failure? The heart orientation of the people: on account of your unclean lewdness, evoking the addiction and patterns of chapter 23. So we should read this failure as 'you would not be cleansed'. So you are right that we should perhaps read the piel as past. But the context is about the reasons for the cleansing not having succeeded, and the cleansing not having succeeded.\
Leviticus 26 has been central enough to our reading that you are right to ask how strong the evidence is. \
First, we have other connections to chapter 4, which we should almost expect given the Biblical tendency towards inclusio as structure, which in turn also connects to Leviticus 26, with a verbal link stronger than this single word. But the strongest link is that word, rot, which is not at all common, so the link between Ezekiel 24, Ezekiel 4, and Leviticus 26 should be considered strong on its own. \
Moreover, as a conclusion to all of these chapters, chapter 24 referring to Leviticus 26 should not be surprising, given how often the various parts of Leviticus 26 have been referenced in earlier chapters. \
Also, this is weaker evidence, but Leviticus 26 does mention the destruction of the sanctuary, right before it mentions the final exile.\
Moreover, the command to Ezekiel not to mourn is paralleled by Jeremiah 16:5-8, which immediately follows language in the previous verse which in turn references some of the other curses of Leviticus 26.\
Lastly, the sign connection between chapter 24 and chapter 12 may also import some Leviticus 26 references.\
But I think the word rot is the single strongest piece of evidence.\
As for reproof, it is entirely possible we have gone beyond what this chapter says. I see more reproof in chapter 33 and 34 than you do, but we will have to leave that for the instances studying those chapters. But ultimately, since we do not know what else Ezekiel said which was not recorded in this book, we lack evidence, so perhaps we should be more cautious. Instead, we should build on the switch from judgement oracles directed at Israel; that is, we have evidence for what you asked, the nature of Ezekiel's prophetic speech changing after the fall.\
Looking more carefully at the evidence this way, I've had to turn to the text of Ezekiel again and again, and the rest of the Bible as well, and enter more fully into what the LORD said through Leviticus 26. So I thank you for asking for more evidence, even though I suggested it.
