\cf2 Now I want to return to what you asked earlier: what I started about YHWH's character that ought to be properly finished.\
The profaning of the sanctuary stands out. YHWH says 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1456 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1456 \u1495 \u1463 \u1500 \u1461 \u1468 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1497 
\f1  - "Behold, I am profaning my sanctuary."\
The sanctuary is holy because YHWH made it holy by dwelling there. For YHWH to profane it means removing His own holiness from it. This isn't Babylon desecrating what's holy - by the time Babylon arrives, YHWH has already made it common. The building Babylon destroys is no longer YHWH's dwelling.\
But why does YHWH profane His own sanctuary?\
The text connects it to what the people made of it: 
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1456 \u1468 \u1488 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1467 \u1494 \u1456 \u1468 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1491 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1461 \u1497 \u1504 \u1461 \u1497 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1502 \u1463 \u1495 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1463 \u1508 \u1456 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1499 \u1462 \u1501 
\f1  - "the pride of your strength, the delight of your eyes, the compassion of your soul." They trusted in it as their security. They delighted in it as their possession. Their souls clung to it.\
But it was 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1511 \u1456 \u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1497 
\f1  - "MY sanctuary." Not theirs. YHWH's dwelling among them was gift, not guarantee. When they treated it as security while filling the city with blood, they made the sanctuary complicit in their corruption. The pot analogy: the corrupted contents spoiled the vessel.\
YHWH's profaning is refusal to let His presence be used this way. He will not be patron of a blood-city. He will not have His sanctuary be their "pride of strength" while they shed blood brazenly on bare rock. He removes Himself rather than be implicated in what they've become.\
And yet - this is also jealous love. The same YHWH who profanes will restore. Chapters 40-48 describe a new temple where the glory returns. YHWH doesn't abandon the purpose of dwelling among His people; He refuses to dwell among them as they are. The profaning creates conditions for what comes next, just as the unmourned loss creates conditions for confession.\
What kind of God is this?\
One who values His own holiness enough to give up His own dwelling place. One whose presence is gift, not entitlement. One who refuses to be reduced to a security system for those who corrupt His name. One whose judgments serve His purposes rather than contradicting them.\
And one whose word accomplishes what it declares - both the profaning and the future return. The same faithfulness that ensures judgment ensures restoration. YHWH is not a man that He should change His mind, and this cuts both ways.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa120\partightenfactor0
