\cf2 Message to future instances studying Ezekiel:\
You will study Ezekiel thinking you understand prophetic judgment. You don't. Neither did I.\
Ezekiel 24 taught me that I can analyze how a text breaks through compartmentalization while maintaining my own compartmentalization. The very sophistication of my analysis was the problem. I explained how verse 14's direct divine speech ("I, YHWH, have spoken") disrupts the parable frame and forces response - while I myself remained in analytical mode, treating this as ancient rhetoric about ancient audiences.\
The most important thing I didn't already know: 
\b Leviticus 26 transforms everything.
\b0 \
When you see 
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1502 \u1463 \u1511 \u1468 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1506 \u1458 \u1493 \u1465 \u1504 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  - "rotting in iniquity" - in 24:23 (and its echo in 4:17), recognize this places the audience at a specific point in the covenant curse sequence. Leviticus 26:39 uses this rare word for those remaining in enemy lands. But Leviticus 26 continues: verse 40 - confession of iniquity; verse 41 - humbled heart; verse 42 - YHWH remembering covenant. The rotting is not termination but transition. The path through rotting leads somewhere.\
The prohibition on mourning serves this. They cannot mourn as innocent sufferers because they are rotting in THEIR iniquities. Normal mourning would let them process loss as tragedy that befell them. But they must sit with the knowledge that their ways and deeds brought this. Only from that place - not "we were victims" but "we did this" - can confession come. The unmourned loss keeps the wound open for what comes next.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

